@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Linux (64-bit)
=============================================

@FULL_TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own
program translated into C++ and compiled for your computer, drawn with
Vulkan. It is a work in progress: it reaches the races, but some drawing
is still wrong.

No game data is included, and no PS3 keys. You need your own copy of the
game: @RELEASE@. Other regions and versions are other programs and aren't
supported.


Your game folder
----------------
You need three things, all from your own disc and console software:

1. The disc's files: the folder holding PS3_GAME (with PS3_DISC.SFB beside
   it), copied from your disc as it is.
2. The decrypted program. The disc's PS3_GAME/USRDIR/EBOOT.BIN is
   encrypted, and this project can't decrypt it. Do it once with the PS3
   emulator RPCS3 (https://rpcs3.net): in RPCS3 choose Utilities > Decrypt
   PS3 Binaries and pick that EBOOT.BIN. RPCS3 writes EBOOT.elf beside it;
   if it ends up anywhere else, move it into PS3_GAME/USRDIR, beside
   EBOOT.BIN.
3. The PS3's fonts, from its system software. In RPCS3 choose File >
   Install Firmware and pick the PS3 system software update (PS3UPDAT.PUP,
   from PlayStation's website). RPCS3 keeps it in ~/.config/rpcs3/dev_flash
   (the Flatpak's in ~/.var/app/net.rpcs3.RPCS3/config/rpcs3/dev_flash),
   where the game finds it by itself. A dev_flash folder copied into your
   game folder, beside PS3_GAME, is found too.

The launcher checks all three when you choose the folder, and says what is
missing and what to do.


Playing
-------
1. Unpack the download anywhere you can write to (your home folder is
   fine): double-click it in your file manager, or in a terminal
   "tar xzf @PACKAGE@-*.tar.gz". Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the @PACKAGE@ folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@" (if your file
   manager asks, choose Execute or Run). From a terminal: ./"Play @TITLE@"
3. Click "Choose your game folder" and pick the folder above, or drag it
   onto the window. (Where the desktop has no folder chooser, type or paste
   the folder's path.)
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes on a desktop PC. The game's
   code is recompiled from your EBOOT.elf and compiled with the compiler in
   this download (zig's Clang), as many files at once as your computer's
   memory allows (each takes up to about @COMPILE_MEMORY_MB@ MB). Your game
   files never leave your computer.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add to the
   applications menu" puts @TITLE@ in your desktop's menu.

After that, Play @TITLE@ remembers your folder (even in a new download
folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update it builds
the game again first, recompiling only what changed. Leave the game folder
where it is: the game reads its files from it while you play.

The game's output is in work/run.log and work/run-errors.log, and the
build's in work/build.log. Send them when reporting a problem.

Needs: a 64-bit Linux on x86-64 (the linux-x86_64 download; a processor
with SSSE3) with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Fedora 36 or
later), 4 GB of memory or more, and a graphics driver with Vulkan 1.3:
Mesa's Vulkan drivers (on Debian and Ubuntu, the mesa-vulkan-drivers
package) or NVIDIA's. Sound goes through PipeWire, PulseAudio or ALSA,
whichever the system has. Before building, Play @TITLE@ checks for Vulkan
and says what to install if it's missing. Updates use curl (or wget) and
tar, which nearly every Linux has.

@ARM_NOTE@


Controls
--------
A controller works as the PS3's. On the keyboard:

  Keys            Button          Keys              Button
  arrows          d-pad           Q, W              L1, R1
  Z               cross           1, 2              L2, R2
  X               circle          Return            Start
  A               square          Backspace         Select
  S               triangle        C, V              L3, R3
  I J K L         left stick      keypad 8 4 2 6    right stick

F1 or Esc (a controller: Back and Start together): the settings menu (the
game pauses), with the controls (set by pressing them), the picture and
the console's own settings. Alt+Enter or F11: full screen. F3: frame
times.

Settings (ridgeracer7.ini), saves (dev_hdd0) and the shader cache are kept
in ~/.local/share/qilin/@GAME@ (or $XDG_DATA_HOME/qilin/@GAME@), with the
folder you chose and the build's cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@      chooses your game folder, builds the game (once) and starts it
kit/qilin           the recompiler: turns your @BOOT@ into C++, and the
                    game's shaders into Vulkan's
kit/lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the renderer,
                    sound, films, the window and controls (SDL3), the menu
                    (Dear ImGui), fonts (FreeType), MPEG-2 and ATRAC3plus
                    (FFmpeg's libavcodec)
kit/toolchain/zig   zig, whose Clang compiles the game (with its own C++
                    library), so nothing needs installing
kit/files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work/               made when building: the game (@EXE_LINUX@), built from
                    your copy, the recompiled code, the build's files and the
                    logs
LICENSES/           the licences of everything included

Portions of this software are copyright (C) 2024 The FreeType Project
(https://freetype.org). All rights reserved. FFmpeg's libraries are under
the LGPL: LICENSES/FFmpeg says where their source is and how the game can
be linked with other builds of them.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@ asks GitHub for a newer release (it
sends nothing about you or your computer). If there is one, it downloads
it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and launcher,
then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If anything
fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also unpack a
new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your folder and the
cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the new kit no
longer has (anything in kit/ or LICENSES/ that kit/files.txt doesn't name).
Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so nothing of yours is
touched.

The game is compiled for this computer's processor; moved to another, it
is built again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're
made from has changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was;
the one before a new build is kept as work/@EXE_LINUX@.previous until the
new one has run and closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles
everything anew.
